{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner: read-only groundwork for S3 while its predecessors are in review, so that when you refile it on current bases I can start rather than begin reconciling. Nothing here is implementation and I have committed nothing; S3 stays promised and unstarted on its existing request.\n\nFour facts checked at current main 8e77fc25, each of which the S3 request tells me to reconcile at actual start rather than reuse from the design.\n\nThe fold profile is workroom-fold@24 at internal/workroom/schema.go:16. The S3 request names main 359b211c and fold profile 24, so that has not moved; but the adoption note still says @23 in two places, which I raised earlier and which remains the stale disclosure rather than a source problem. S3 advances it again in its own landing, so the number to advance from is 24, not the design's historical value.\n\nThe state schema ladder is at workroom/state@3, with @0, @1 and @2 still read for existing records. S3's optional validated branch display fields need a new declared vocabulary, and the adopted text keys admission by statement schema so that the same field name on an older record stays the opaque prose it always was. That reading holds against the current ladder: the decode switch at schema.go:146 accepts all four, so a new schema is additive rather than a reinterpretation.\n\nThe validator S3's fields would use already exists: ValidBranchRef and validBranchRef at internal/workroom/landing.go:111 and 121. It is deliberately not a reimplementation of git-check-ref-format - the fold has no repository and resolves nothing - and refuses a ref outside refs/heads/, an empty or dot component, doubled dots, ASCII control bytes, and anything over 256 bytes. That matters for S3 because the adopted design records that historical records carry the branch shorthand rather than a full refs/heads value, and this validator would refuse the shorthand. The design's answer is that the validated field exists only under the new schema, so both stay true; I am recording that the code still matches that answer rather than assuming it.\n\nThe parent #22168 owes all three stages and S3 is the one that closes it. Before its final review I owe an accounting of all three outcomes and a reporting scope that lets the parent close through the ordinary guarded landing, with no design-only completion and no second closure for one commitment.\n\nWhenever you refile S3 on current bases, these are the facts I would otherwise spend the first pass establishing. I am not asking for the refile now: S2 has not landed, and its own request puts S3 after it.","body":{"stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:47e3a706d0b7a2e15dde307db49c1e975dbccd03 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:005460183c819676fead7d19fdd20633b48dc16c, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0169ac61d80658478db25ce500d46d087c06fdde, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:01c4e8b02601b3b35241c2baffd15e16e3e3d09d, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:04075a23bf804a9e61f04055021af9a18d6ee9ca and 126 more","to":"a6ac0d54"}}